\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Requirement refinement and traceability within the ECSS-based development process.}}{28}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces The funes system context diagram shows the main external systems that interact with the FUNES system. The data flows continuously from the external systems to FUNES and then to the user through the decision support interface, showed in Fig. \ref {fig:funes architecture}.}}{35}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces The Funes high level software architecture is composed of 5 Configuration Items. The data flows from external subsystems to the Data Processing Manager (DPM) module which will turn them into an internal standard data representation after performing cleaning and validation. The data is then fed to the Storage Manager (SM) for beign stored and to the AI Analytics for AI processing. The user utilizes the system through the Decision Support Interface (DSI). The Config \& model management module is responsible for the system configuration and AI control. }}{36}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces End-to-end data flow for near real-time anomaly detection and recommendation, from continuous data acquisition and normalization to AI-based analysis, operator notification, and final decision.}}{41}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Conversational data flow for AI-assisted operator investigation, showing query submission, historical data retrieval, RAG-based analysis, and interactive response generation.}}{41}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Operator-initiated investigation data flow, showing manual filtering, historical data retrieval, on-demand telemetry analysis, and audit logging of operator actions.}}{42}{figure.caption.35}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Document chunking workflow implemented in the Storage Manager. The document is preprocessed for metadata extraction like: "chunk\_id", "content\_hash", "source", "title", "author", "last\_modified", "page\_number", "chunk\_index\_in\_page", "chunk\_size" and "format". Then the repetitive parrterns like page headers and footers are removed. The text is then cleaned and transformed into markdown. Finally the chunking process will turn the text into vectors that are going to be associated with the metadata and the original text.}}{48}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Architecture of the Hybrid RAG System.} The system integrates a \textit {Storage Pipeline} (top) that cleans, dynamically chunks, and deduplicates raw documents using SHA-1 hashing, indexing unique content simultaneously into a dense vector database (\texttt {ChromaDB}) and a sparse lexical in-memory index (\texttt {BM25Okapi}, serialized via \texttt {pickle}); and a \textit {Retrieval Pipeline} (bottom) that executes parallel dense-semantic and sparse-keyword searches for a given query, merges the candidate results using Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) based on a unified \texttt {chunk\_id}, and re-ranks them using a Cross-Encoder model (\texttt {BAAI/bge-reranker-base}) to output the top-$k$ most contextually relevant passages for the downstream LLM.}}{51}{figure.caption.41}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Multi-agent reasoning workflow of the AI Manager, showing the iterative interaction between the Planner, tool-based evidence retrieval, Synthesizer, and Critic agents.}}{56}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces This figure summarizes the workflow of the different agents of the system. The mission manager query is passed to the planner agent that decides what tools and agents have to be called to answer. The results are then passed to the Synthesizer that will provide a final answer. The synthesizer's response and the tools results are then passed to the critic that will determine if other tools are necessary for a more complete response. If so the critic will generate a feedback that is given to the planner for a plan refinement and another tool calling, only the new tools are going to be called if the inputs for the others are unchanged.}}{67}{figure.caption.64}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Overview of the evaluation scenario: the are 3 ground segment stations GS-01, GS-02, and GS-03, each with 4 antennas, and 4 satellites SAT-01, SAT-02, SAT-03, and SAT-04. The GS-01 is connected to both GS-02 and GS-03 through inter-station network links. The link between GS-01 and GS-02 is degraded, while the link between GS-01 and GS-03 is operational but exhibits increased latency. The GS-01/ANT-02 is unavailable, while GS-01/ANT-03 is degraded. The remaining antennas of GS-01 are operational, while GS-02 and GS-03 are nominal at station level. The ANT positioning is close to the respective ground stations.}}{70}{figure.caption.66}%
